window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][25] = "<p><strong>Other threats.</strong> <em>Ramping the split mid-test:</em> if the share of traffic in the variant changes over time and behavior varies over time, the pooled comparison can be Simpson's paradox (C12.7); analyze by period or keep the split constant. <em>Instrumentation differences</em> between arms (a new event logged only in the variant). <em>Seasonality or an external event</em> during the test, which affects both arms equally but can change who shows up. <em>Carryover:</em> a user's earlier experience (a previous test) influences their behavior now; keep tests from the same user population separate or randomize independently and check interactions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The checklist catches the failures that make a clean-looking result wrong. <strong>When not to run all checks.</strong> Prioritize SRM (always), the over-time pattern (for any visible change) and interference (for anything social or two-sided).</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-5-primary-and-guardrail-metrics-ship-hold-or-roll-back-d\"></span>\n<h4>C14.5 Primary and guardrail metrics; ship, hold or roll-back decision rules</h4>\n<p>Decide before the data arrive what you will do under each result. FW-C14-05 is the table.</p>\n<p><strong>One primary metric.</strong> The metric the decision rests on, tied to the problem the change addresses (C05, FW-C05-06). A primary metric chosen after seeing which one moved is a result found by searching (<a href=\"#gl-multiple-comparisons\">multiple comparisons</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Metrics that must not get worse: speed, errors, support contacts, cancellations, revenue per user, safety signals. For each, set a <strong>margin</strong>: the largest harm you would accept, written as a number (&quot;support contacts must not rise by more than 3%&quot;). A guardrail passes if the interval for the change lies within the margin, not merely if the point estimate looks small.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary metrics.</strong> Explain the mechanism (did clicks rise because more people started, or because the same people clicked more?). They inform but do not decide, and with many of them, some will look significant by chance, so treat surprises as hypotheses.</p>\n<p><strong>The decision rule.</strong> A template:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ship</em> if the lower end of the primary interval is at or above the smallest worthwhile effect, and every guardrail's interval is within its margin.</li>\n<li><em>A looser, common variant</em> ships if the interval excludes zero and the point estimate is at least the smallest worthwhile effect. The strict version above needs a larger sample than a test powered only to detect the target effect, because the lower end of the interval, not the estimate, must clear the threshold; say which version you are using.</li>\n<li><em>Hold or extend</em> if the primary interval straddles the threshold (not enough data) and guardrails are fine: collect more, or decide on judgment with a staged rollout.</li>\n<li><em>Do not ship, or roll back</em> if the primary interval lies below the threshold, or a guardrail's interval breaches its margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>If the rule seems wrong when the result arrives.</strong> Do not rewrite it to fit. Follow it, note why it felt wrong, and change the rule for next time; if the stakes justify it, rerun with the better rule. Changing the rule after seeing the data is the experimental version of moving the goalposts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A rule written first turns the readout into a decision and prevents motivated reading. <strong>When not to be rigid.</strong> If the result reveals something the rule did not anticipate (a harm outside the guardrails), the right move is to pause and investigate, and to say so.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-6-when-you-cannot-randomize\"></span>\n<h4>C14.6 When you cannot randomize</h4>\n<p>Sometimes you cannot randomize: the change is global (a price change for a whole market), the units cannot be separated, the change has already shipped, or randomizing is unacceptable. FW-C14-06 lists approaches in order of strength, with what each assumes. They are at awareness depth: know the idea, the assumption and the main risk, and know when to call a data scientist.</p>\n<p><strong>Holdouts.</strong> A <strong>holdout</strong> is a group deliberately kept from a change (or a set of changes) for a period so that its outcome serves as the comparison, often over a long horizon. A long-term holdout of, say, 1% of users who do not get any of a quarter's launches measures their cumulative effect and whether early gains faded. The cost is the benefit forgone by the holdout, which is small if the group is small.</p>\n<p><strong>Geographic or market tests.</strong> Treat some cities or regions and compare with matched others. The weakness is that few units (a handful of cities) make the comparison noisy, since each city has its own shocks. Use many matched pairs rather than one against one.</p>\n<p><strong>Difference-in-differences.</strong> <strong>Difference-in-differences</strong> is an estimate of an effect as the change over time in a treated group minus the change over time in a comparison group, which removes shared trends under the assumption that the groups would have moved in parallel without the change. Take a promotion launched in one city (invented). Conversion in the treated city went from 5.0% to 5.6%, a before-and-after gain of 0.6 points. In a comparison city with no promotion it went from 5.0% to 5.3%, so something (a season, a trend) lifted both. The estimate of the promotion's effect is (5.6 − 5.0) − (5.3 − 5.0) = 0.6 − 0.3 = +0.3 points, half the naive figure. The assumption is <em>parallel trends</em>: without the promotion the treated city would have moved like the comparison city. Check it on the weeks before the launch: if the two lines moved together then, the assumption is more credible. The risk is that city-specific shocks are not removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Switchback tests.</strong> Alternate the treatment on and off over time periods for the same market (treated Monday, control Tuesday...), used where users interact and cannot be split, such as dispatch or pricing in a marketplace. Needs enough periods, and attention to carryover between them.</p>\n<p><strong>Staggered rollouts as natural experiments.</strong> When a feature rolls out to groups at different times for operational reasons, early and late groups can be compared, provided the order was not chosen on the basis of the outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Before and after.</strong> The weakest: anything that changed at the same time is confounded. Use only with a control, or as a first look.</p>\n<p><strong>Observational methods.</strong> Matching and regression adjustment (comparing similar users who did and did not get the change), and methods such as synthetic control, can be useful, and they rest on strong assumptions that unmeasured confounders do not matter. Say that, and call it weaker evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming the rung you are on and what it cannot rule out is the honest way to use weaker evidence. <strong>When not to over-engineer.</strong> If a clean experiment is possible, run it.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-7-experimentation-at-scale-platforms-variance-reduction-\"></span>\n<h4>C14.7 Experimentation at scale: platforms, variance reduction, long-term holdouts</h4>\n<p>Large product companies run many experiments at once on a shared platform <code>[V: Tang et al., KDD 2010; Kohavi and Thomke, HBR 2017]</code>. A PM should know the main ideas.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Platforms and layers.</strong> Companies have published how they run thousands of overlapping experiments by assigning each event (for example a web search) to one experiment in each of several independent layers, so that tests of unrelated changes do not interfere <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Tang et al., KDD 2010]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic checks.</strong> Platforms run SRM checks and other health checks automatically and flag results that look too good, in the spirit of Twyman's law (C12.7).</li>\n<li><strong>Variance reduction.</strong> Using a user's pre-experiment behavior as a covariate reduces noise, so tests can reach the same power with fewer users or less time (the approach commonly called CUPED).</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term holdouts.</strong> Keeping a small group on the old experience for months to measure whether gains persist and how many small wins add up. This addresses the problem that short tests overstate effects that fade, and that a series of small wins may not sum.</li>\n<li><strong>Most ideas do not win.</strong> Practitioners at large companies have reported that only a minority of tested ideas improve their target metric: Kohavi and Thomke write that at Google and Bing only about 10% to 20% of experiments generate positive results, and that at Microsoft as a whole one-third are effective, one-third neutral and one-third negative <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, HBR, 2017]</code>, which is why testing is valuable and why a PM should be comfortable with null and negative results.</li>\n<li><strong>Opportunity cost.</strong> An experiment uses traffic, time and attention. Prioritizing which tests to run is a PM decision (C15).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Knowing the vocabulary lets you talk to a data scientist and discuss tradeoffs without pretending to build the platform. <strong>When not to go deeper.</strong> In an interview, a sentence on layers, automatic checks and long-term holdouts is enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-8-reading-readouts-with-conflicting-metrics\"></span>\n<h4>C14.8 Reading readouts with conflicting metrics</h4>\n<p>Readouts rarely show every metric moving the right way. FW-C14-07 gives a hierarchy.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Validity first.</strong> Is there a sample-ratio mismatch? Did the test run as planned? If not, stop: the metrics do not matter.</li>\n<li><strong>Guardrails next.</strong> Does any guardrail's interval breach its margin? If so, the pre-set rule says do not ship as is, and the work is to understand why.</li>\n<li><strong>Then the primary metric.</strong> Compare its interval with the threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Then secondary metrics</strong> to explain the mechanism.</li>\n<li><strong>Then segments.</strong> Effects can differ by group (new versus existing users, platforms). Treat a surprising segment as a hypothesis to test, since slicing finds noise (C12.7); an effect in a pre-specified segment of interest deserves more weight.</li>\n<li><strong>Then the horizon.</strong> Is the effect stable over time, or decaying? Is there a long-term holdout?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Common conflicts.</strong> <em>Primary up, guardrail down:</em> weigh the size of each in the same units (dollars, users), look for the mechanism, and consider a version that keeps the gain and removes the harm. <em>Short term up, long term unknown:</em> ship in stages with a long-term holdout. <em>Overall flat, segments opposite:</em> the average hides two effects, a case for targeting. <em>Many secondary metrics, one primary flat:</em> resist declaring a win on a secondary.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The order protects against the most expensive mistakes first. <strong>When not to apply it mechanically.</strong> If a guardrail breach is tiny in absolute terms and the rule's margin was clearly too tight, say so and recommend a re-test with a better margin, not an override.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-9-answer-architecture-for-experiment-design-questions\"></span>\n<h4>C14.9 Answer architecture for experiment-design questions</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the design answer with a time budget: decision and hypothesis, unit, metrics, size and duration, validity, decision rule, and what to do if you cannot randomize.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-10-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C14.10 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives six worked examples: designing a test end to end, diagnosing a sample-ratio mismatch, a novelty effect, a readout with conflicting metrics, interference in a social product and a difference-in-differences estimate. Section 11 adds eight questions with model answers, including an Apple question on testing a once-per-user flow, and one each set on a Google, a Meta and a Netflix product.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The six-question statistics check (FW-C12-01), the interval reading card (FW-C12-03), the significance triage (FW-C12-04), the rule-of-16 sketch (FW-C12-05), the trap detector (FW-C12-07), the success-metric template (FW-C05-06) and the good-news check (FW-C13-08) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-01 · The experiment design card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a change to evaluate and a decision that depends on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a one-page card: decision and hypothesis, unit, randomization, treatment and control, exposure, metrics, analysis, duration and decision rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the change can be assigned randomly and traffic is enough to power the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a card adds up-front effort and saves a rerun.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a card with no decision; a unit different from the analysis unit; a primary metric chosen after the fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the shipping-estimate test in Worked example 1, and the designs in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parts: <strong>Decision · Hypothesis · Unit · Randomization · Treatment/Control · Exposure · Primary/Guardrails/Secondary · Analysis · Duration · Decision rule.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-02 · The ladder of causal evidence · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a claim that a change caused an outcome and the evidence offered.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the rung the evidence sits on, what it cannot rule out, and the next stronger evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> stronger evidence is available at an acceptable cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> climbing costs time and money; sometimes a lower rung is enough for a cheap, reversible decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> presenting a correlation as an effect; dismissing all non-randomized evidence; not naming the confounder.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the webinar counterexample in C14.1 and the notification case in CS-C14-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rung</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>What it cannot rule out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Anecdote</td>\n<td>Everything</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Correlation in the data</td>\n<td>Confounders; reverse causation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Before and after</td>\n<td>Anything else that changed over time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Comparison with an analyst-chosen control</td>\n<td>How the control was chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Matched or modeled control; difference-in-differences</td>\n<td>Unmeasured confounders; parallel-trends failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Randomized experiment</td>\n<td>Invalid setup; generalization limits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-03 · The validity checklist · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a designed or completed experiment.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of threats with the check run and the remedy, and a verdict (valid, valid with limits, invalid).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> assignment and exposure logs are available.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> checks take time; skipping them risks acting on an invalid result.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> reading metrics before checking the split; checking only SRM; ignoring interference in a two-sided product.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the mismatch in Worked example 2 and the novelty check in Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Remedy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample-ratio mismatch</td>\n<td>Compare observed split with planned (z-test; strict alarm, p &lt; 0.001)</td>\n<td>Find the cause; fix; rerun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Novelty or primacy</td>\n<td>Effect by week since first exposure; new versus existing users</td>\n<td>Run longer; use the stable level; long-term holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interference</td>\n<td>Do units affect each other (social, marketplace)?</td>\n<td>Cluster randomization; switchback; measure spillover</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contamination</td>\n<td>Share of units seen in both arms</td>\n<td>Stable identifiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peeking</td>\n<td>Was the stopping rule fixed?</td>\n<td>Fixed horizon or sequential method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ramping</td>\n<td>Did the split change mid-test?</td>\n<td>Constant split or analyze by period</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instrumentation</td>\n<td>Are events logged the same in both arms?</td>\n<td>Fix logging; A/A test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-04 · The sample-size and duration planner · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> baseline rate, smallest worthwhile effect, daily eligible traffic, exposure share.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> users per arm, duration in whole weeks, a triggered-versus-diluted comparison, and options if the test is too long.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> two arms, a proportion metric, 80% power, 5% two-sided significance, independent units.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a sketch decides feasibility; important tests deserve a proper power analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> choosing the effect to fit the traffic; analyzing everyone when few are exposed; ignoring weekly cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1, and the dilution arithmetic in C14.3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps as in C14.3. Dilution: <strong>effect among everyone ≈ exposure share × effect among the exposed</strong>; analyze the exposed (triggered) and require identical triggering in both arms.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-05 · The decision-rule table · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the smallest worthwhile effect, guardrails with margins, and the costs of shipping wrongly or not shipping.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a written rule for ship, hold or extend, and do not ship or roll back, fixed before the test starts.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> margins can be set from the cost of harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a fixed rule can feel rigid; changing it after the data is worse.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> no guardrail margins; choosing the primary metric afterward; overriding the rule silently.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the rule in Worked example 1 and its use in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>Action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary interval ≥ threshold; guardrails within margins</td>\n<td>Ship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary interval straddles threshold; guardrails fine</td>\n<td>Hold or extend; or staged rollout on judgment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary interval below threshold</td>\n<td>Do not ship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any guardrail interval breaches its margin</td>\n<td>Do not ship as is; investigate; rollback if live</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-06 · The no-randomization toolkit · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a change that cannot be randomized and the data available.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a chosen method (holdout, geographic test, difference-in-differences, switchback, staggered rollout, before and after with control), its assumption, a check on the assumption, and a note on how much weaker the evidence is.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> a credible comparison group or period exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> weaker methods are cheaper and rest on assumptions that cannot be fully tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a single treated and a single control unit; no pre-period check; calling the result proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the promotion estimate in Worked example 6, and the Apple setup question in IQ-C14-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Fits</th>\n<th>Key assumption</th>\n<th>Check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-term holdout</td>\n<td>Cumulative effects of many launches</td>\n<td>The holdout is a random small group</td>\n<td>Same as an experiment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geographic test</td>\n<td>Market-level changes</td>\n<td>Matched markets behave alike</td>\n<td>Pre-period similarity; many units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Difference-in-differences</td>\n<td>A change in one group over time</td>\n<td>Parallel trends</td>\n<td>Pre-period trends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Switchback</td>\n<td>Interacting units (marketplaces)</td>\n<td>Little carryover between periods</td>\n<td>Period length; carryover test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Staggered rollout</td>\n<td>Rollout order not tied to outcomes</td>\n<td>Order as good as random</td>\n<td>How order was chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before and after</td>\n<td>A first look</td>\n<td>Nothing else changed</td>\n<td>A control</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-07 · The conflicting-metrics readout · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a readout with a primary metric, guardrails, secondary metrics and segment cuts.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a verdict in the order validity, guardrails, primary, secondary, segments, horizon, with a decision and the reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the decision rule was set before the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a strict order protects against motivated reading and can ignore context.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> declaring a win on a secondary metric; overriding a guardrail without saying so; trusting a surprising segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the readout in Worked example 4, and IQ-C14-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Order: <strong>Validity → Guardrails → Primary → Secondary → Segments → Horizon → Decision.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c14-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C14-08 · The low-frequency-event design · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a change to something users do rarely or once (device setup, sign-up, a yearly renewal), so outcomes arrive slowly.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a design that uses proxy metrics measured soon after, cohorts over a longer horizon, a holdout, and qualitative evidence, with the link between proxy and outcome stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the proxy plausibly predicts the real outcome; the cohorts are comparable.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> proxies arrive fast and may not predict the outcome; waiting for the outcome is slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating a proxy as the goal (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>); a test too short to see the outcome; ignoring that each user is measured once.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the device-setup test in IQ-C14-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) name the real outcome and its delay; (2) choose proxies that arrive early and predict it, and state how you would validate the link; (3) run the test in cohorts so each user is in one arm; (4) hold out a small group for the long outcome; (5) add qualitative evidence (watch sessions, support contacts); (6) decide on the proxy and the guardrails, and check the long outcome later.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c14-the-experiment-design-answer-for-a-30-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The experiment-design answer, for a 30-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision and hypothesis</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>What we will do under each result; the hypothesis and the mechanism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit and randomization</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The unit with a reason; assignment; exposure and trigger; contamination risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>One primary, guardrails with margins, a few secondary metrics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size and duration</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Baseline, smallest worthwhile effect, users per arm, whole weeks; what if too long</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validity</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>SRM, novelty, interference, peeking; the checks and remedies that matter here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision rule and readout</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ship, hold, do not ship; how conflicting metrics are read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If you cannot randomize</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The method, its assumption and how much weaker the evidence is</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives the metric or the unit, spend the time on validity and the decision rule. If you run behind, keep the decision rule, the unit and the SRM check. Treat questions as steering and rejoin the card.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> State the decision first: &quot;What would we do if it wins, and if it loses?&quot; State the rule in advance: &quot;I'd ship only if the lower end of the interval is above a 0.5 point lift and support contacts stay within 3%.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd randomize [unit] 50/50, measure [primary] with [guardrails], run [n] weeks, and ship if [rule]. The threats I'd watch are [two], and I'd change my plan if [condition].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c14-type-design-a-test\"></span>\n<h4>Type: design a test</h4>\n<p>Use the design card (FW-C14-01). Spend the middle on the unit, the metric and the decision rule.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-type-read-a-result\"></span>\n<h4>Type: read a result</h4>\n<p>Use the validity checklist first, then the readout order (FW-C14-07), then the triage from C12 (FW-C12-04).</p>\n<span id=\"c14-type-you-can-t-randomize\"></span>\n<h4>Type: you can't randomize</h4>\n<p>State the rung of the ladder (FW-C14-02), choose a method from the toolkit (FW-C14-06), name its assumption and the check, and say how much weaker the evidence is.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-type-rare-or-slow-events\"></span>\n<h4>Type: rare or slow events</h4>\n<p>Use the low-frequency design (FW-C14-08): proxies, cohorts, a holdout and qualitative evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-type-the-short-version-10-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (10 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Decision, unit, primary metric and guardrail, sample size sketch, SRM check and decision rule. Protect the decision rule.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why not just compare before and after?&quot;</td>\n<td>They test the counterfactual</td>\n<td>Name what else changed over time; propose a control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What is your unit?&quot;</td>\n<td>You did not choose one</td>\n<td>Choose with a reason; say you analyze at that unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What's your primary metric?&quot;</td>\n<td>You listed many</td>\n<td>Choose one tied to the problem, with guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How long will it run?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a sketch</td>\n<td>Use the rule of 16; round to whole weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The split is 51/49.&quot;</td>\n<td>A possible SRM</td>\n<td>Compute the z-score; set the strict alarm; look for the cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The lift is shrinking each week.&quot;</td>\n<td>Novelty</td>\n<td>Look by time since exposure; use the stable level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if the groups affect each other?&quot;</td>\n<td>Interference</td>\n<td>Cluster randomization or switchback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;You can't randomize.&quot;</td>\n<td>A different question</td>\n<td>Name the method, the assumption and the weaker evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c14-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All six examples are <code>Illustrative</code>; the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each follows the design card (FW-C14-01) and is written as notes you could say aloud; a spoken answer is shorter.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-1-designing-a-test-end-to-end-a-shipping-cost\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Designing a test end to end: a shipping-cost estimate on the cart page (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A shop wants to show an estimated shipping cost on the cart page, hoping more people finish buying (invented). Building the estimate needs a shipping-rate service that costs money to run.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision and hypothesis.</strong> The decision is whether to keep the service running. Hypothesis: people abandon carts because shipping is a surprise at the last step; showing the cost early removes the surprise and raises the share who buy.</p>\n<p><strong>Unit and randomization.</strong> The user, assigned 50/50 by a stable hash of the user ID with this test's own salt, so a user sees one version on every visit and device where they are logged in. Exposure: the change is visible only on the cart page, which 40% of visitors reach. Log &quot;reached the cart&quot; identically in both arms and analyze only users who did (a triggered analysis).</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: the share of cart-reaching users who buy (baseline 10%). Guardrails: checkout error rate (must not rise by more than 0.2 points), page speed at the 95th percentile (no more than 50 milliseconds slower), and support contacts about shipping per 1,000 orders (no more than a 3% rise). Secondary: whether more users start checkout, and average order value.</p>\n<p><strong>Size and duration (FW-C14-04).</strong> The smallest effect worth the service's cost is +1 point (10% to 11%). The rule of 16 gives 16 × 0.10 × 0.90 ÷ 0.01² = 14,400 cart-reaching users per arm (an exact calculation gives about 14,750). With 20,000 visitors a day, 8,000 reach the cart, which is 4,000 per arm per day, so the sample arrives in 3.6 days. I would run two full weeks anyway: two weekly cycles, and time to see whether the effect fades.</p>\n<p><strong>Validity plan.</strong> Check the split before reading any metric. Look at the effect by week and for new versus returning users. No interference (one person's cart does not affect another's). Fix the duration in advance and look once at the end, with a dashboard watch on the guardrails only.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision rule (FW-C14-05), written before the test.</strong> Ship if the interval for the lift excludes zero and the estimate is at least +0.5 points, and every guardrail's interval is within its margin. Extend if the estimate is at least +0.5 but the interval includes zero. Do not ship if the upper end of the interval is below +0.5 points, or if a guardrail breaches its margin.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Every row of the card was filled before launch, and the rule was fixed before the data. The sample was small because the analysis was limited to the people who could be affected.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-2-a-sample-ratio-mismatch-that-created-a-fake\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — A sample-ratio mismatch that created a fake win (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A test assigns 1,000,000 users 50/50 (invented). The assignment log shows 500,000 in each arm. The analytics readout shows 500,000 users in control and 492,000 in the variant, and a conversion of 5.00% in control against 5.08% in the variant, a &quot;+1.6% lift.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Check the split first (FW-C14-03).</strong> The analytics counts total 992,000, so the expected count per arm is 496,000. The control has 500,000 and the variant 492,000, a gap of 4,000 from expectation. The standard deviation of an arm's count is √(992,000 × 0.25) ≈ 498, so the gap is about 8 standard deviations: far beyond the strict alarm (p below 0.001) and certainly not chance. A sample-ratio mismatch.</p>\n<p><strong>Where did 8,000 users go?</strong> The assignment log says each arm got 500,000, so 8,000 variant users were assigned and then not logged. The variant page has a heavy new script and loads more slowly (the team's own note on the change). Users who leave before the analytics event fires are never counted. Those are likely the lowest-intent users, who rarely buy.</p>\n<p><strong>What it does to the result.</strong> Suppose both arms truly convert at 5.00%, so each has 25,000 purchases from 500,000 users. In the readout, the variant's 25,000 purchases are divided by only 492,000 logged users: 25,000 ÷ 492,000 = 5.08%. The entire &quot;+1.6% lift&quot; (5.08 against 5.00) comes from dropping the 8,000 users who would not have bought. Another way to see it: the variant is not better, and it loses 1.6% of its users before they are counted.</p>\n<p><strong>What to do.</strong> Do not read the conversion result. Compare assignment (server-side) with analytics, and use the assignment log as the denominator: purchases per assigned user, 5.00% in both arms. Then ask the more important question the artifact exposed: the variant is slow enough to lose 1.6% of visitors before the page finishes loading, which is itself a harm worth measuring as a guardrail (page speed, bounce). Fix the cause and rerun or analyze on assigned users.</p>\n<p><strong>What a smaller gap means.</strong> A split of 501,000 against 499,000 on 1,000,000 users is 2 standard deviations (p ≈ 0.046), which chance produces about once in 22 tests. It is a reason to look, not to throw the test away; hence the stricter alarm.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A clean-looking win was produced by differential loss of users. The split check, run before the metrics, caught it.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-3-a-novelty-effect-the-lift-that-kept-shrinki\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — A novelty effect: the lift that kept shrinking (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a streaming service of the kind Netflix operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A redesigned homepage row for choosing what to watch is tested for six weeks (invented). The weekly lift in viewing starts per member, treatment against control:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n<th>5</th>\n<th>6</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lift</td>\n<td>+6.0%</td>\n<td>+3.5%</td>\n<td>+1.8%</td>\n<td>+1.2%</td>\n<td>+1.0%</td>\n<td>+1.0%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lift decays and settles near +1%. The average over six weeks is about 2.4%; the average of the first two weeks is 4.75%.</p>\n<p><strong>Split by tenure.</strong> Among existing members, the lift was +7.5% in week 1 and +0.6% in week 5. Among new members it was +2.0% in week 1 and +1.9% in week 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading it.</strong> The decay is a novelty effect: existing members, who knew the old layout, explored the new one at first and then returned to their habits. New members, who have no old habit, show a stable +2%. The effect to plan around is the stable level, about +1% overall and +2% for new members. A test stopped after two weeks would have reported nearly +5%, almost five times the settled value.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Report the effect at the stable level (weeks 4 to 6), not the average, and say that the early weeks are not representative. Ship, because a stable positive effect with no guardrail harm is a win, and keep a 2% long-term holdout (C14.6) to confirm the effect persists over months. If the layout had been designed mainly for new members, consider showing it first to them.</p>\n<p><strong>Mirror image.</strong> If the lift had started negative and recovered, that would be change aversion, and the same remedy applies: watch the trend by time since first exposure and judge by the settled level.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A single number for the whole test hid the pattern; the trend by week and the split by tenure showed which part was durable.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-4-a-readout-with-conflicting-metrics-illustra\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — A readout with conflicting metrics (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A test of a redesigned checkout ran two weeks (invented). The pre-set rule: ship if the primary interval is at or above +0.2 points and every guardrail is within its margin; the support-contacts guardrail margin is +3%. The split passes the sample-ratio check.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>95% interval</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purchase rate (primary), control 5.00%</td>\n<td>+0.30 points</td>\n<td>+0.10 to +0.50</td>\n<td>Lower end 0.10 is below the +0.2 threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support contacts per 1,000 orders (guardrail), control 20.0</td>\n<td>+4%</td>\n<td>+0.5% to +7.5%</td>\n<td>Upper end 7.5% exceeds the +3% margin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Page speed, 95th percentile (guardrail)</td>\n<td>+10 ms</td>\n<td>−5 to +25 ms</td>\n<td>Within the 50 ms margin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Apply the order (FW-C14-07).</strong> <em>Validity:</em> passes. <em>Guardrails:</em> support contacts breach the margin: the interval's upper end is 7.5%, above 3%. <em>Primary:</em> +0.30 points looks good, though the interval's lower end is below the threshold. So under the rule as written, the verdict is not to ship as is.</p>\n<p><strong>Put numbers on it.</strong> Over a week with 1,000,000 visitors, control has 50,000 orders and the variant 53,000 (a +0.30 point lift is 3,000 more orders). At a $12 margin per order, the gain is about $36,000. Support contacts: control 1,000 (20 per 1,000 orders); variant 53,000 × 20.8 ÷ 1,000 ≈ 1,102, about 102 more contacts. At $5 per contact, that is about $510. In dollars the harm is small against the gain.</p>\n<p><strong>So is the guardrail wrong?</strong> Possibly, and the discipline is not to decide that after seeing the result. The margin exists because contacts are a signal of confusion, which can cost more than the handling cost: refunds, chargebacks, lost trust. Look at the mechanism (a secondary metric): what are the extra contacts about? If they are about an unexpected charge or a confusing label, the redesign has a flaw that can be fixed, and the primary gain may be partly built on misleading users.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Hold the full launch. Fix the cause of the extra contacts (clear the label), run a short confirmation test or a staged rollout with the guardrail monitored, and record that for next time the margin should be stated in dollars and in user-experience terms together. Note too that the primary interval's lower end (+0.10) is under the +0.2 threshold: even without the guardrail, the strict rule would say extend. Do not ship on the point estimate alone.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The order (guardrails before the headline) and a pre-set rule kept a good-looking primary result from overriding a warning sign, and quantifying both sides showed that the answer was to fix the flaw, not to choose between them.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-5-interference-when-control-users-are-affecte\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Interference: when control users are affected too (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a social product of the kind Meta operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A new suggestion feature is meant to help people find friends to connect with. The outcome is friend requests sent per user per week.</p>\n<p><strong>A user-level test.</strong> Users are randomized individually. Result: treated users send 10% more requests than they did before; control users, who do not have the feature, send 2% more, because treated friends now send them requests and reply, which prompts them to send more. The measured difference is about +8%.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it is misleading.</strong> The control group is not a picture of &quot;no feature.&quot; Some of the treatment effect has leaked into it through the network. The +8% understates what would happen if everyone had the feature, since the control's rise is partly the feature's doing.</p>\n<p><strong>A cluster test.</strong> Randomize whole communities (groups of about 100 closely connected people) so that most friends are in the same arm. Result: treated communities send 11% more requests than control communities, which are flat. The cluster estimate, +11%, is closer to the effect of launching to everyone.</p>\n<p><strong>The marketplace mirror.</strong> Take a discount tested on individual buyers who compete for the same limited supply of sellers (invented). Treated buyers book 12% more, and control buyers book 3% fewer, because the treated took the available supply. The measured gap is +15%, but if everyone had the discount, no one could take supply from anyone, and the total effect might be about +6%. Interference here overstates the effect.</p>\n<p><strong>What to say in an interview.</strong> &quot;Wherever units affect each other (a social network, a marketplace, a shared resource) a user-level test can give a biased answer in either direction. I'd randomize clusters, such as communities or markets, or alternate the treatment over time in a market, accept fewer units and wider intervals, and check the size of spillover.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The direction of the bias depends on whether interaction spreads benefit (understatement) or competes for a fixed pool (overstatement).</p>\n<span id=\"c14-worked-example-6-no-randomization-a-price-change-in-one-mark\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 6 — No randomization: a price change in one market (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A subscription service raised its price from $10 to $11 in one market, with no test (invented). New sign-ups per 1,000 visits went from 40 to 34 in that market. A comparison market with no price change went from 40 to 38 over the same weeks, because of a seasonal dip.</p>\n<p><strong>The naive reading.</strong> Before and after in the treated market: 34 − 40 = −6 sign-ups per 1,000 visits. Revenue per 1,000 visits fell from 40 × $10 = $400 to 34 × $11 = $374, a loss of $26, which looks like a clear mistake.</p>\n<p><strong>Difference-in-differences (FW-C14-06).</strong> The comparison market shows that without a price change, sign-ups would have fallen by 2 (to 38) because of the season. The price effect on sign-ups is (34 − 40) − (38 − 40) = −6 − (−2) = −4 per 1,000 visits, which is a 10.5% fall (4 of 38) for a 10% price rise. Revenue per 1,000 visits: with the price change, 34 × $11 = $374. Counterfactual without it, 38 × $10 = $380. The effect on first-period revenue is −$6, about −1.6%, much smaller than the −$26 the naive comparison suggests, so the price change is about revenue-neutral on first-period revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Assumptions and checks.</strong> <em>Parallel trends:</em> absent the price change, the treated market would have moved like the comparison market. Check the weeks before the change: if the two markets' sign-ups moved together then (say 40, 40, 41, 40 against 40, 41, 40, 40), the assumption is more credible. <em>Shocks that hit only one market</em> (a competitor's launch, a local holiday) are not removed. <em>One treated and one comparison market</em> gives no way to estimate market-level noise.</p>\n<p><strong>What is not covered.</strong> First-period revenue is not lifetime value. A higher price may change who joins and how long they stay, and that takes months to see. Also the measured sign-up fall excludes any effect on existing subscribers' churn.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Treat the result as weak evidence that the price rise is roughly neutral on first-period revenue. Before extending it to other markets, use several matched markets (for example, ten treated and ten comparison), include a long-term holdout market, and watch retention. Say plainly that this is a lower rung of evidence than a randomized test (FW-C14-02).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The naive before and after overstated the harm four-fold, and the comparison market was what made the difference visible.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;How would you test whether a new 'smart reply' suggestion feature improves messaging?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c14-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd run an A/B test: half of users get smart replies and half don't. I'd measure whether they send more messages, and ship it if the number goes up.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no decision, unit or metric definition. &quot;More messages&quot; is a weak and gameable measure: suggestions could raise message counts with worse conversations. There is no guardrail, duration or rule, and no thought about the person who receives the replies.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First the decision: ship the feature, or not, depending on whether it helps people communicate. I'd randomize by user, 50/50, with a stable assignment. The primary metric would be tied to the problem the feature solves: time to reply to a message, among people who received messages, since the feature is meant to make replying faster and easier. Guardrails: conversation quality (replies per conversation, the share of conversations that continue), reports of unwanted or awkward replies, and the share of messages people edit or delete after sending.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd size it with the rule of 16 on the reply-time metric, run at least two full weeks, fix the duration in advance and look once at the end. I'd check the split before looking at any metric, and look at the effect by week for novelty, since a new suggestion bar is attention-grabbing at first.</p>\n<p>&quot;Decision rule: ship if reply time falls by a worthwhile amount, with an interval above the threshold, and no guardrail breaches its margin.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c14-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;Messaging is a two-sided product, so there is interference: if I randomize individual users, a treated user's quick reply lands in a control user's inbox and changes how they respond. The user-level result may be biased, so I'd randomize clusters of people who message each other, such as conversation groups, and accept a wider interval. I'd also check for spillover directly by comparing control users who talk to many treated users with those who talk to few.</p>\n<p>&quot;The metric needs care (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05): if I optimized message counts, suggestions could inflate them with shallow replies. So I'd treat reply time as the primary metric and conversation depth as a counter metric. I'd also look at who benefits and who does not: people who write long, personal replies may feel their style is being replaced; a segment cut by message length would show it.</p>\n<p>&quot;On the decision, there is a long-term question a two-week test cannot answer: whether people's own writing changes or whether recipients start to find replies generic. I'd keep a long-term holdout of 1% and track how recipients rate conversations.</p>\n<p>&quot;What would change my plan: if clusters of conversation groups are too large to give enough units, I'd fall back to user-level with a spillover check and be explicit that the estimate is biased in one direction. And I'd consult privacy and safety teams early, since suggestions are generated from message content.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c14-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States the decision, unit, one primary metric and guardrails</td>\n<td>Sees interference in a two-sided product and randomizes clusters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sizes the test and fixes the duration and rule</td>\n<td>Names a counter metric for gaming and a long-term holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checks the split and the effect over time</td>\n<td>States what would change the plan and brings in privacy early</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c14-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google engineers have published papers on infrastructure for running many overlapping experiments at once <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Tang, Agarwal, O'Brien and Meyer, KDD 2010]</code>. Candidates commonly report experiment design and interpretation questions at large scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: state the unit and the primary metric early, and be ready for follow-ups on interference and on how you would read conflicting metrics.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon is reported to be among the companies that run very large numbers of online controlled experiments each year, according to articles by experimentation leaders <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, HBR 2017: Amazon among companies running more than 10,000 online controlled experiments a year]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions that probe whether you start from the customer's problem and can say what you would measure <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: tie the primary metric to a customer outcome, and name guardrails that protect customer trust.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta researchers have published on designing online experiments and on bias from interference between connected users <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Eckles, Karrer and Ugander, Journal of Causal Inference, 2017]</code>. Candidates commonly report experiment questions in which users affect each other <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: when the product is social, raise interference unprompted and propose cluster-level randomization.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Leaders of Microsoft's experimentation platform have written widely, including the Bing headline case in this chapter <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, HBR 2017, and the Bing headline case]</code>. Practical consequence: show the instinct to distrust results that look too good, and name the checks (sample ratio, logging, A/A) before celebrating.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple has described using differential privacy to learn about usage while limiting what it learns about individuals <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Machine Learning Journal, Learning with Privacy at Scale (2017); Apple privacy control page]</code>. Third-party guides report that experiment prompts at Apple concern products used rarely or once, such as device setup, where outcomes arrive slowly <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: when each user does a thing once, say how you would use proxies, cohorts and holdouts, and how measurement limits shape the design.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes technical posts about its experimentation practices, including tests of how titles are presented <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog posts on experimentation and resilience testing]</code>. Candidates commonly report data-informed prompts on presentation and engagement <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate short-term clicks from long-term retention, and watch for novelty.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Experiments in payments touch money, fraud and authorization rates, and businesses depend on stable behavior <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's reasoning from public product descriptions; no source checked]</code>; Stripe publishes documentation for developers who integrate its products <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe documentation]</code>. Practical consequence: use guardrails on fraud, failed payments and disputes, and say how you would limit exposure of a change that can affect money.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c14-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>&quot;Just run an A/B test&quot;</td>\n<td>No decision, unit or metric</td>\n<td>Fill in the design card first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Before-and-after called an effect</td>\n<td>The comparison is across time with no control</td>\n<td>Name what else changed; add a control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Randomizing one unit and analyzing another</td>\n<td>Users randomized, sessions analyzed</td>\n<td>Analyze at the unit of randomization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Many metrics, no primary</td>\n<td>A list of ten, any of which could win</td>\n<td>One primary, guardrails, secondary as explanation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>No decision rule</td>\n<td>The rule appears after the results</td>\n<td>Write the rule before the test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Not checking the split</td>\n<td>Metrics read before the allocation</td>\n<td>Check sample-ratio first, with a strict alarm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Stopping when significant</td>\n<td>&quot;It hit significance on day 4&quot;</td>\n<td>Fix the horizon; or use a sequential method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ignoring novelty</td>\n<td>One average over the whole test</td>\n<td>Plot by week and by tenure; use the settled level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Ignoring interference</td>\n<td>A social or two-sided product tested by user</td>\n<td>Cluster randomization or switchback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Treating a weak method as proof</td>\n<td>A difference-in-differences with one city each</td>\n<td>State the rung and the assumption; use many units</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c14-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>The counterfactual</strong> (<code>MM-counterfactual</code>). Every causal claim is a claim about a world you cannot see: what would have happened without the change. Ask &quot;compared with what?&quot; until you can name the comparison and say how it was formed. A randomized control is the best available stand-in; every other comparison deserves a sentence on how it could differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Pre-commitment</strong> (<code>MM-precommit</code>). Decide the rule before the evidence arrives, the way a sailor ties himself to the mast before hearing the sirens. A decision rule, a primary metric and a stopping point written down first keep you from reading the data to fit what you hoped.</p>\n<p><strong>Too good to be true</strong> (<code>MM-twyman</code>, see C12.7). Large and surprising results are more often bugs than discoveries. Run the split check, look for logging differences and check that the effect makes sense mechanistically, before believing or celebrating. The Bing case shows the right reflex: an alert for implausible results, and a check that the result was real.</p>\n<p><strong>Spillover</strong> (<code>MM-spillover</code>). A treatment given to one unit often reaches others, through a network, a shared inventory or a shared price. Whenever units are connected, ask whether the control is really untreated, and which way the bias runs: benefits that spread understate the effect, competition for a fixed pool overstates it.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> The experiment depends on assignment and logging. Ask who owns the assignment service, whether assignment is logged on the server before exposure, how exposure is triggered, how the split is monitored, and how a test is switched off. Many invalid tests are logging failures. Share the design card so engineers can say what cannot be measured.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> A test says whether something worked, not why. Pair it with a usability session on the change before the test (to catch confusing designs) and a few interviews or recordings after (to explain the result). Ask design what metric the new design is meant to move, and check that it is the primary one.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Bring the data scientist the decision and the smallest worthwhile effect before the design. Ask them to set the sample size, the variance reduction and the monitoring, and to review the readout against the pre-set rule. Ask what the analysis cannot show, and how interference and novelty were checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Ethics, privacy and safety.</strong> Experiments on people can harm them: confusing prices, manipulative designs, tests on emotional content or on children. Involve privacy, legal and safety teams early for changes that touch sensitive content, money or vulnerable users, and set guardrails on harm as well as on business metrics. If a test would be unacceptable to disclose to the people in it, rethink it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c14-checklist-before-you-launch-or-trust-an-experiment\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you launch or trust an experiment</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is there a decision, a hypothesis and a rule for what we do under each result?</li>\n<li>Is the unit of randomization sensible, and do I analyze at that unit?</li>\n<li>Is there one primary metric, with guardrails and margins set in advance?</li>\n<li>Is the test sized for the smallest worthwhile effect, with a duration in whole weeks?</li>\n<li>Did I check the split before reading metrics?</li>\n<li>Did I check the effect over time and for interference?</li>\n<li>Did I fix the stopping point, and avoid stopping when the result first looks significant?</li>\n<li>If I could not randomize, did I name the method, its assumption and the weaker evidence?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c14-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c14-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C14-01 · The notification default that did nothing · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A fitness app's data show that users who have push notifications turned on retain at 60% after 30 days, against 30% for users who have them off. A product manager proposes to switch notifications on by default for all new users, expecting retention to rise 30 points.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A two-person team, a default that can be changed back, and a review of the feature in two weeks. App-store reviews have recently mentioned too many notifications.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The comparison is observational: users chose their setting. An analyst proposes a randomized test of the default for new users. With 10,000 new users in each arm, the results after 30 days are: retention 40.0% with the default off and 41.0% with it on, a lift of +1.0 point with a 95% interval of about −0.4 to +2.4. In the first week, uninstalls were 6.5% with the default off and 8.0% with it on, a rise of 1.5 points with an interval of about +0.8 to +2.2.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The correlation (60% against 30%) invites the belief that notifications cause retention. As an observable question: &quot;If we turn notifications on for people who did not choose them, does retention change?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> On the ladder of evidence (FW-C14-02), the first comparison is rung 2, a correlation: users who turned notifications on were already more engaged, and that engagement, a confounder, explains both the setting and the retention. The randomized test is rung 6 and shows what the default itself does. The retention effect is small (+1 point) and not distinguishable from zero, while the cost (more uninstalls in the first week) is clearly different from zero. The gap between the correlation (30 points) and the experiment (1 point, uncertain) is the size of the selection effect. The pre-set rule would have been: ship only if retention rises by a worthwhile amount with the uninstall guardrail within a margin of +0.5 points; the uninstall interval's lower end (+0.8) breaches that margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Ship the default for everyone</td>\n<td>The expected 30-point gain</td>\n<td>More uninstalls; a user-trust cost; no retention gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Do not ship the default; ask for permission at a moment when the user has seen value</td>\n<td>Retention among people who choose</td>\n<td>A smaller reach of notifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Run a second test of a permission prompt after the first workout</td>\n<td>Learning what raises opt-in without hurting trust</td>\n<td>Two more weeks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A would have been a mistake that the correlation recommended. B respects the user and uses the confounder (engaged users opt in) as a signal. C costs time and tests the better idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Do not ship the default (B), and run C. Primary metric for C: the share of new users who opt in after the first workout. Guardrails: week-one uninstalls and 30-day retention. Counter metric: notification mute rate after 30 days, which would show whether the notifications that people allowed are wanted.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the permission prompt after the first workout raised opt-in from 35% to 52% (invented) with no change in uninstalls, and retention for the test group rose by 1.2 points with an interval of −0.3 to +2.7 (invented), a small effect in the same direction. The default was not shipped.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the randomized test had shown a +10 point lift, the default would have been worth shipping despite the uninstalls, by the rule's cost-benefit, and the lesson would have been that the correlation understated or matched the effect. If the sample-ratio check had failed, the result would have been set aside and rerun. The lesson is the method: a correlation among people who chose a setting is a poor guide to what happens when you assign it.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c14-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C14-02 · Bing, 2012: the shelved headline change · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): an article by Ron Kohavi, who led Microsoft's analysis and experimentation team, and Stefan Thomke, a Harvard Business School professor, &quot;The Surprising Power of Online Experiments,&quot; Harvard Business Review (September–October 2017), read in full in its reprint (a first-hand account by practitioners, primary for the practice and for the numbers they report, which are the company's own and were not independently checked). The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In 2012 an employee on Microsoft's Bing search engine proposed a change to how ad headlines were displayed. The article says it would take only a few days of an engineer's time but was one of hundreds of ideas proposed, and program managers judged it a low priority. It sat for more than six months. Then an engineer, seeing that the cost of coding it was small, launched a simple online controlled experiment to assess it. Within hours the new headline variation was producing abnormally high revenue, which triggered a &quot;too good to be true&quot; alert. Alerts like that usually signal a bug, and here, per the article, analysis showed a real revenue increase of 12%, which on an annual basis would be more than $100 million in the United States alone, without hurting key user-experience metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Hundreds of ideas competed for limited engineering time, and people's judgment of their value was unreliable. Revenue from ads can come at the cost of the user's experience, so guardrails on experience were needed. The platform had an automated check for results that look implausibly good. The headline figure is an annualized projection from the test, not a measured annual total.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: a revenue increase of about 12% in the experiment; an annualized value of more than $100 million for the United States; no harm to key user-experience metrics; an alert within hours of launch. The article also states that Microsoft and several other leading companies, including Amazon, Booking.com, Facebook and Google, each run more than 10,000 online controlled experiments a year, and that at Google and Bing only about 10% to 20% of experiments generate positive results, while at Microsoft as a whole one-third prove effective, one-third are neutral and one-third are negative <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, 2017]</code>. The article does not describe the effect's behavior over months, and it does not say whether this particular result was replicated; elsewhere it advises replicating surprising results (it calls this Twyman's law, &quot;any figure that looks interesting or different is usually wrong&quot;) and describes rerunning a 2013 color experiment with 32 million users <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, 2017]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The organization's way of deciding which ideas to build (managers' judgment) misjudged the value of one idea by a very large margin. The problem was one of prioritization under uncertainty: the cost of testing the idea was far less than the cost of the debate, and the prior view of its value was wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Through this chapter's frameworks, the case shows several things. The randomized controlled test is the top rung of the ladder (FW-C14-02): no argument about confounders was needed. The measurement had a primary metric (revenue) and guardrails (user-experience metrics), the structure of FW-C14-05, so a win in revenue was not bought with a hidden loss in experience. The &quot;too good to be true&quot; alert is the practice of the mental model in section 9: a large result is first treated as a possible error (a logging or assignment fault), and the check here came back clean. The alert also shows that an experimentation platform's automated checks, such as the sample-ratio check, are part of trustworthiness (C14.7).</p>\n<p>For prioritization (C15), the lesson is that when testing is cheap, testing can beat ranking by opinion, since the value of some ideas is only revealed by the test. It would be a mistake to read the case as &quot;every shelved idea is a hidden win&quot;: the same practitioners report that most ideas do not help, and this one is famous because it is rare. A PM should draw the narrower lesson, which is that low-cost tests of cheap ideas are a good bet, and that the expected value of an idea is uncertain enough that confident rankings are fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits.</strong> The $100 million is an extrapolation from a test period to a year, and the sources used here do not show whether the effect persisted without fading (the novelty question, C14.4), whether it was the same in other markets, or how the revenue measure related to long-term user satisfaction. The article reports the result as a success, and a single case, however well documented, is a survivor.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of Microsoft's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Leave the idea shelved on managers' judgment</td>\n<td>Engineering focus on higher-ranked ideas</td>\n<td>The idea's value stays unknown; in this case a large gain was missed for months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Ship the change without a test</td>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>No evidence of effect on revenue or experience; risk of an unseen harm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Run a quick controlled experiment with revenue as the primary metric and experience guardrails</td>\n<td>Evidence for a small cost</td>\n<td>A few days of engineering time and some traffic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> C costs little and gives the information that A and B lack. A is the cheapest and, in this case, the most expensive in forgone revenue. B is fast and leaves harm and benefit unmeasured.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, an engineer launched the experiment, and the result was analyzed; the article describes the outcome as the best revenue-generating idea in Bing's history, although its value was underappreciated until the test; it does not say in so many words that the change shipped, which the description implies <code>[V: Kohavi and Thomke, 2017]</code> <code>[inference]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: a 12% increase in revenue in the test, more than $100 million a year projected for the United States, no harm to key experience metrics, and the case becoming a standard example in arguments for experimenting widely.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the alert flagged a real bug (an assignment or logging error), the &quot;12%&quot; would have been an artifact, which is exactly why the check comes first. Had the effect been a novelty that faded within weeks, the annualized figure would have been far too high, and a long-term holdout would have caught it. Had a guardrail on experience shown harm, the decision would have been to hold. The method to carry away: make tests cheap, fix the primary metric and guardrails before launching, treat surprising results as possible errors first, and say which part of a large number is measured and which is projected.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eight questions: two Beginner, three Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the design card (FW-C14-01). Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and user behavior and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round; a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c14-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C14-01 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> We launched a redesigned checkout last week and conversion went up. Why not just compare before and after?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;That is fine, the number went up.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names that other things change over time, so the comparison is confounded, and proposes a control.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies a seasonal or concurrent effect, offers a method that works without randomization, and says what evidence level the answer reaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> A before-and-after comparison answers &quot;what changed?&quot; and not &quot;what did the launch change?&quot; Anything else that moved over the same weeks is mixed into the number: a season, a promotion, a competitor, a traffic mix change, or regression to the mean if we launched because conversion had been low.</p>\n<p>An example (invented). Conversion was 5.0% the week before launch and 5.4% the week after: a gain of 0.4 points. But a market where we did not launch the redesign went from 5.0% to 5.3% over the same weeks, perhaps because of a seasonal rise. The best estimate of the redesign's effect is the difference of the two changes: 0.4 − 0.3 = +0.1 points, a quarter of the naive figure. That is a difference-in-differences estimate (C14.6), and it relies on the assumption that the two markets would have moved alike without the launch.</p>\n<p><em>What I'd do.</em> If the launch can still be randomized, the best option is to keep a holdout: put a random 5% or 10% of users on the old checkout for a few weeks and compare. If it has already shipped everywhere, use a comparison group (a market, a platform or a delayed rollout), check that the two groups moved together before the launch, and say that the evidence is weaker than an experiment because unmeasured differences between the groups could remain.</p>\n<p><em>How I'd say it.</em> &quot;I'd not call the 0.4 points the effect. I'd compare with a control, which suggests about 0.1 points, and I'd tell the team it is a rung-5 estimate, not a randomized one.&quot; What would change my mind: if the comparison market's pre-launch trend differed from ours, the estimate would be unreliable and I'd look for a better comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What method would you use to estimate the effect without randomization? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A comparison group that did not get the change over the same period. If the redesign was launched in one region first, compare its change in conversion with the change in regions that had not yet launched: if the launch region rose from 5.0% to 5.4% and the others from 5.0% to 5.3% because of a promotion, the estimated effect is about 0.1 point, not 0.4. This difference-in-differences estimate needs a similar trend before launch, which I would check on the prior eight weeks. A staggered rollout or a 5% holdout gives a better design if it is still possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What level of evidence does the answer reach? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Weaker than a randomized test. It rests on the assumption that the launch and comparison groups would have moved together without the change, and I would say so. I would report the estimate with an interval, show the pre-launch trends so that the reader can judge the assumption, and note what could break it, such as a regional event. If the decision is large and irreversible, I would ask for a holdout or an experiment next time; for a reversible change, a careful quasi-experiment may be enough.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c14-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C14-02 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An experiment was planned 50/50. The readout shows 5,100 users in one arm and 4,900 in the other. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's close enough,&quot; or &quot;the test is broken.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes how unusual the gap is and knows the sample-ratio check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes a borderline from a clear mismatch, sets a strict alarm threshold, and lists causes and actions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Compute how unusual it is. There are 10,000 users, so the expected count per arm is 5,000. The standard deviation of an arm's count is √(10,000 × 0.25) = 50. A count of 5,100 is 100 above, which is 2 standard deviations (p ≈ 0.046). By chance, that happens about once in 22 tests, so on its own it is not alarming. The usual practice is a stricter alarm for this check, for example p below 0.001, which at this sample size would be a gap of about 165 users, or 3.3 standard deviations.</p>\n<p><em>So what do I do?</em> Not discard the test. Note it as borderline and look at the pattern: is the gap stable across days, or does it grow? Check the assignment log against the analytics counts, and whether the arms are logged identically. If more data keep the gap at about 2%, the standard deviation shrinks and the mismatch becomes clear, and then I treat the test as invalid.</p>\n<p><em>Contrast with a clear case.</em> If 1,000,000 users were assigned and the readout showed 508,000 and 492,000, the gap is 8,000 against a standard deviation of 500: 16 standard deviations. That is not chance. Something differs between arms in who is counted (a bot filter on one arm, a slower variant that loses users before logging, a failed redirect). I would not read any metric from such a test, because the missing users are rarely typical.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> For 5,100 against 4,900: continue, check the logs, and decide when the test ends. For the clear mismatch: stop, find the cause, fix and rerun. What would change my view: if the assignment log itself showed 5,100 and 4,900, the randomizer would be at fault, which is rarer and more serious.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What causes a mismatch like this and what would you check? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Assignment and logging problems, not luck. Typical causes: a bug in the randomization or in who is eligible, bots or bad traffic filtered after assignment in one arm, a redirect or a slower page in one arm that causes more users to leave before they are counted, and different logging between versions. I would check the split by platform, browser, country and time to see where the imbalance sits, and whether the mismatch appears at assignment or only at the first event. I would not read any effect until the cause is found.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Is a 2 standard-deviation gap alarming, and what if it persists at larger scale? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;At 10,000 users, 5,100 against 4,900 is two standard deviations, about p = 0.046, which happens by chance about once in 22 tests, so it is a watch item, not an alarm. The usual alarm is much stricter, for example p below 0.001. But if the same 51/49 split persists at 1,000,000 users, the arms are 510,000 and 490,000, 20 standard deviations from even, which is certainly a defect. So I would re-check as the sample grows, and treat a persistent small imbalance as a bug.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c14-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C14-03 · Intermediate · analytics · AN · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design an experiment for a new layout of a search results page.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A/B test it and look at clicks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Chooses the unit, the metrics (with guardrails), the size and the duration, and decides on a rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that clicks can mislead when a layout answers the question directly, picks success and satisfaction measures, handles query-type differences and novelty, and sets guardrails on speed, advertisers and long-term return.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information. The decision: ship the new layout if it helps people find what they want. Hypothesis: the layout makes the answer easier to reach.</p>\n<p><em>Unit.</em> The user, not the query, because a person sees the layout across many queries and learns it; randomizing queries would show one person different layouts and blur learning effects. Assignment by a stable hash of a user identifier, with a consistent identifier for signed-out users where possible.</p>\n";
